# Source: Exercise list "ICT3101 Exercises - Deductive Verification" (HoareLogic Exercises.pdf), problem 5 d), and Lecture Quiz 2 (solutions), first question.
id: minus-two
title: A loop that subtracts two
topic: Loops
level: 2
source: Exercise list "HoareLogic Exercises", problem 5 d; Lecture Quiz 2
tags: while, invariant, variant

task:
  Give a proof outline and a loop invariant. Give a variant for the loop.

  The precondition is $x = n$ with the condition $n > 0$ added, so that $x$
  starts with a positive value. The triple is also valid without $n > 0$,
  but then the invariant must also cover start values below $-1$.

given:
  Loop condition B: $x > 0$

pre: x = n && n > 0
post: x <= 0

program:
  {P}
  {I}
  while (x > 0) do {
    {I && x > 0}
    {R1}
    x := x - 2
    {I}
  }
  {I && !(x > 0)}
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: The invariant I
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  If $x$ starts even, the values at the loop head go down by two and the
  loop stops at $0$. If $x$ starts odd, the last iteration goes from $1$ to
  $-1$. So every value of $x$ at the loop head is at least $-1$.
set: I = x >= -1
text:
  The loop rule with consequence has three premises, with the loop
  condition $B$ being $x > 0$: initialisation $P ==> I$, preservation
  $\{I && B\}$ `x := x - 2` $\{I\}$, and conclusion $I && !B ==> Q$.

step: P implies I
part: Initialisation
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: P, I
text:
  The invariant must hold when the loop is reached. No statement comes
  before the loop, so the precondition $P$ must imply $I$.
  >>> «P» ==> «I»
vc: x = n && n > 0 ==> x >= -1
why:
  From $x = n$ and $n > 0$ we get $x > 0$, so $x >= -1$.

step: R1, the weakest precondition of x := x - 2 for I
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: x := x - 2
text:
  One iteration must keep the invariant:
  >>> {«I» && «x > 0 : B»} x := x - 2 {«I»}
  We calculate the weakest precondition of the body for $I$. We substitute
  the assigned variable ($x$) with the assignment expression ($x - 2$) in
  $I$.
calc:
  wp(x := x - 2, I)
  = (x >= -1)[x <- x - 2]     // I
  = x - 2 >= -1      // x is replaced by x - 2
  = x >= 1           // add 2 to both sides
set: R1 = x >= 1

step: I and B imply R1
part: Preservation
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: I && x > 0, R1
text:
  By the consequence rule, the triple holds when $I && B$ implies $R1$,
  computed in the previous step.
  >>> «I» && «x > 0 : B» ==> «R1»
vc: x >= -1 && x > 0 ==> x >= 1
why:
  For integers, $x > 0$ is the same as $x >= 1$.

step: I and not B imply Q
part: Conclusion
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: I && !(x > 0), Q
text:
  When the loop stops, $I$ holds and $B$ is false. This must imply the
  postcondition $Q$.
  >>> «I» && «!(x > 0) : ¬B» ==> «Q»
vc: x >= -1 && !(x > 0) ==> x <= 0
why:
  $!(x > 0)$ is $x <= 0$, which is $Q$ itself.

step: The variant is not negative
part: Termination
rule: WHILE-TOTAL
focus: I && x > 0
text:
  The body decreases $x$ by two, and the loop runs only while $x > 0$. So
  we take the variant $E = x$. The rule WHILE-TOTAL has two premises. The
  first one: $E$ is not negative when an iteration starts.
  >>> «I» && «x > 0 : B» ==> «x >= 0 : E >= 0»
vc: x >= -1 && x > 0 ==> x >= 0
why:
  $x > 0$ gives $x >= 0$.

step: The body decreases the variant
part: Termination
rule: WHILE-TOTAL, ASSIGN, WP
focus: x := x - 2
text:
  The second premise: the body keeps $I$ and makes $E$ smaller. $E0$ is a
  logical variable for the value of $E$ when the iteration starts.
  >>> {«I» && «x > 0 : B» && «x = E0 : E = E0»} x := x - 2 {«I» && «x < E0 : E < E0»}
  We calculate the weakest precondition of the body for the
  postcondition of this triple. We substitute $x$ with $x - 2$.
calc:
  wp(x := x - 2, x >= -1 && x < E0)
  = (x >= -1 && x < E0)[x <- x - 2]     // I is x >= -1, E is x
  = x - 2 >= -1 && x - 2 < E0     // x is replaced by x - 2
  = x >= 1 && x - 2 < E0          // add 2 to both sides of x - 2 >= -1
vc: x >= -1 && x > 0 && x = E0 ==> x >= 1 && x - 2 < E0
why:
  For integers, $x > 0$ is $x >= 1$. From $x = E0$ we get
  $x - 2 = E0 - 2 < E0$.
result: The triple is valid, and the loop terminates.

summary:
  The postcondition $x <= 0$ does not say where the loop stops. It stops
  at $0$ when $n$ is even, and at $-1$ when $n$ is odd.
